well hello there watching the press  preview a first look then at what is on  the front pages in the next half hour  we'll see what's making the headlines  with the journalist and author christina  patterson and the columnist at the  article ali mirage great to see both of  you as ever though we'll start with the  front pages and the observer focusing on  the prime minister's deepening twin  political crises amid pressure on covert  deals and the row over so-called tory  sleeves reaching new heights  the sunday times is reporting on an  investigation into the conservative  party offering seats in the house of  lords to multi-millionaire donors who  have given large sums to the party  study express leads with an exclusive on  the foreign secretary liz truss's vision  for britain as it declares it's time to  deliver jobs of the future  people power is essential because  politicians just talk and do nothing  reads the scotland on sunday as cop 26  climate protests take place in glasgow  and around the world  the telegraph has seen leaked papers  which show that ministers are prepared  to walk away from major eu research  programs if brussels plays hardball over  the northern ireland borders protocol  and the mail leading with an exclusive  as they say that ministers plan on  bringing back tests and quarantine for  those who refuse to take the booster jab  why every mom should get jab headlines  the sunday people with a story about a  baby girl whose mum has died from covet  just as she was born  the mirror leading with a nurse who  claims to have had a relationship with  david fuller the man who abused corpses  while working in a morgue  while the star on sunday has a story  about strictly come dancing star  aj adodu and his new partner on the show  so christina patterson and ali mirage  are here um quite a mix of headlines has  to be said um but ali talk us through  the observer first of all with the  continuing repercussions of that vote in  in parliament last week in the  subsequent resignation of owen patterson  well yes anna this is uh this uh debacle  continues with the observer now  reporting that  the organizations that owen patterson  was  lobbying for actually secured up to  500 million pounds worth of contracts  government contracts so this story  rumbles on and it's a very very sorry  saga for the government and particularly  the prime minister here to have got  himself into  this is uh people will remember back in  the mid 1990s the the cash for questions  saga they'll also remember the the cash  for peerages saga under tony blair so  governments of various stripes have got  themselves into trouble on this but this  is beginning to  now  the government has to be careful about  having this whiff around it of just  making up the rules as it wishes john  major was very vociferous earlier today  saying that the government is  in certain essences politically corrupt  uh and uh was being very very strong on  this obviously he's a remainer doesn't  like the government because they're  brexiteers but you also have to go back  to the fact that jonathan jones the  government uh head of the legal service  uh resigned in 2020 over the fact that  the government was going to ride  roughshod over the withdrawal agreement  and you had alex allen the adviser on  the ministerial code resigned on the  back of the prime minister ignoring his  recommendations over bullying uh  obligations in relation to pretty patel  so the government's got form on this and  the prime minister needs to get a grip  uh because if you're a backbencher in  the tory party right now you'll be  scratching your head wondering why you  would go and back for the government  again when you're going to have your  legs cut from underneath you within 24  hours when they throw you under the bus  yes i mean certainly sir john major  calling the the whole episode shameful  uh you know other uh leader writing  pieces suggesting the pillars of  democracy being chipped away and yet  christopher hope from the uh daily  telegraph saying actually the government  was trying to do the right thing here  first of all giving mps um  a right of reply effectively and there  was a lot of sympathy for mr patterson  obviously what after what happened to  his his wife the two therefore conflated  together so wherever your sympathies lie  um where does it leave both um the  question marks now over the government  and what they do about this vote that  they've had they need to unpick or do  they christina  well i think the fact that i mean they  they are certainly in a pickle and it's  much more than a pickle actually i'm  sure anybody would have sympathy with  someone whose wife has committed suicide  that's an absolute tragedy and anyone's  heart would go out to owen patterson or  anyone in that situation i think  unfortunately the maybe it's the grief  the most charitable light one can cast  on this is that the grief has  overshadowed his judgment because he was  found to have committed egregious was  the word used in the report egregious uh  breaches of the of the code there is  very little question the evidence is  very damning and um  you know  what's not clear is whether they were  just wanting to get owen patterson off  the hook or if they were actually  wanting to overhaul the whole system  possibly it has been argued because  boris johnson knows that he will face is  likely to face further investigations  over curtin gate  and gold wallpaper gate and all the  other gates that involve unbelievably  expensive and dubiously funded interior  decor at downing street and maybe he was  trying to get  you know make sure that he didn't get a  harsh judgment on that we don't know  what we do know  is that the word corruption and sleaze  has been used extensively this week and  i think i was probably one of many many  people who felt that john mage's  interview this morning was quite  cathartic i went to bed profoundly  depressed the other night when  parliament had voted to abolish the  rulemakers and and decide they were  going to make the rules themselves that  was a real nadir even in a country that  has seen a lot of nadirs in recent years  and to have a formatory prime minister  who himself knows a thing or two about  sleaze and who started the nolan  committee and the nolan principles of  public life as a result of that to hear  his words talking about the corruption  of this government it was some relief  because i think some of us feel we've  been going mad in the last few years  yeah and certainly many people pointing  out that sir john major is no fan of  boris johnson or anything that he has  achieved namely brexit of course so  that's been certainly a part of the mood  music after that interview today um  let's move on to the sunday time shall  we a new tori sleazer hours donors who  pay three million pounds get seats in  the house of lords well we've had a  statement from the conservative party  but um you know tell us about this story  because you know it's not the first time  it has been suggested that this might be  what  happens well i'm surprised it's news  anna  i i'm really surprised that it's news i  mean  parties of all political persuasions for  a long time have been accused of  doling out uh peerages to people who  give cash you've got a situation at the  moment where lord fowler the speaker of  the house of lords has been pleading  with successive prime ministers to limit  the number of people going into the  upper chamber there are now over 800  peers if vladimir putin was stuffing his  the duma with his cronies we'll be  screaming blue murder this is meant to  be the mother of all parliaments and we  have a situation here where  mates of the prime minister successive  prime ministers go back goes back to  labor as well in 0607 there was a police  investigation into the labour party on  their part also um supposedly doling out  cash for peerages so this is not just a  conservative party issue you've got a  situation where you can end up in the  legislature for life by giving money uh  to the party look if you want to reward  political uh donors there's an honor  system to do that there are other ways  of doing it you don't have to give them  a seat in legislature for life and  frankly in its current form i think the  lord's is becoming increasingly  untenable it plays a very important  function in our democracy the house of  lords it's good that they're not elected  but in its present form it's really  coming into distribute and becoming a  bit of a joke so this is a long-running  saga i think it needs to be looked at  thoroughly and it's something that  affects all parties and needs to be  addressed yeah i mean you've picked on  this the article or the paragraph rather  in the santa terms article that i  noticed which is the alleged use of  seeds in the lords as an arm of party  fundraising is particularly  controversial because unlike other  honors like knighthoods peers fulfill an  important role in the legislative  process as a check and balance for new  laws so as you say it has implications  uh the story then that uh the  conservative party was systematically  offering seats in the lords to a select  group of multi-millionaire donors who  pay more than 3 million to the party 3  million pounds  they have issued the statement to us  tonight we do not believe that  successful business people and  philanthropists who contribute to  political causes and parties should be  disqualified from sitting in the  legislature so that's that i want to  very quickly if you don't mind christina  go to  the protests we've seen in glasgow and  elsewhere today um as uh negotiators um  hardly head back for week two after a  day of rest on sunday at cop26 climate  conference um it's been fascinating  hasn't it the people power on the  streets um are they still  as relevant as the money men inside who  are  seeking out  means of investing now in renewable  energy do you think  well i think they have helped to shift  the dial i i'm not a fan of extinction  rebellion i don't like their methods i  don't like the fact that for example  some of their protests have involved  stopping cleaners in the east end  getting to work by uh you know leaping  on tube trains and things like that but  it but you know without these global  protests we wouldn't be where we are now  and people like boris johnson would not  have had their damascene conversion from  severe climatic skepticism to  apparently a huge enthusiasm for action  but not obviously enough action to stop  him leaping straight on a private jet to  get back to a lovely dinner at the  garrett club with charles moore so um  you know  what we need is for people to actually  do what they say they will do and not be  hypocrites then it would be lovely if  our government could perhaps set an  example on that front rather than you  know still jetting around the world i  think boris johnson was on a private jet  just a a couple of weeks ago in fact  possibly last week i think he's on  private jets most weak actually and  given that there are generally seats  available on other flights it doesn't  seem like you know a great look but but  broadly speaking there have been moves  on deforestation which could be very  significant the very fact that india has  committed to to  net zero by 2070 is huge obviously  that's a long way away but it's much  better than that might have been  expected so that there is some good news  here and now we need uh we need to take  the action which will be uncomfortable  and difficult for many many people and  expensive is one of the key points so  you know today  i think yesterday the governor of the  bank of england was saying gas is going  to go up partly because of the cutting  back on fossil fuels and you know it's  all very well to say i'm in favor of  action but when it hits your wallet  that's often a different matter

